// Launches this extension in a second VS Code window, for when the `client` folder is the one
// open. (Opening the repository root instead uses the root .vscode/launch.json, which is local to
// each machine - /.vscode/ is in .gitignore, so this file is the one everybody gets.)
//
// Three things here were wrong and are worth naming, because each fails silently:
//
//   * `main` in package.json is ./dist/extension.js, written by esbuild through the `bundle`
//     script. The build task was `tsc -watch`, and tsconfig writes to out/. So F5 type-checked the
//     sources and then launched an extension whose entry point it had not built. dist/ is in
//     .gitignore, so on a fresh clone it is absent altogether and the Extension Development Host
//     comes up with no `main` to load - which is what a launch that "crashes as the window opens,
//     with no .as file involved" looks like. Where dist/ did exist, F5 ran whatever was bundled
//     last, which is worse: the code runs, it is just not the code on disk.
//
//   * `outFiles` pointed at out/, the tsc output, while the code being executed was dist/. Nothing
//     for a breakpoint to bind to.
//
//   * `--extensionDevelopmentPath` was ${workspaceFolder}/client. This file is read when `client`
//     IS the workspace folder, so that resolved to client/client.
//
// `build` below chains compile and bundle in that order. Both are needed and neither replaces the
// other: esbuild does no type checking, and tsc also writes out/test/, which the integration suite
// loads directly rather than through the bundle. That is written down at the top of esbuild.mjs.
{
	"version": "0.2.0",
	"configurations": [
		{
			"name": "Run Extension",
			"type": "extensionHost",
			"request": "launch",
			"args": [
				"--extensionDevelopmentPath=${workspaceFolder}"
			],
			"outFiles": [
				"${workspaceFolder}/dist/**/*.js"
			],
			"preLaunchTask": "build"
		}
	]
}
